1: # Arbitrary-fan flop: the exact spin cap and the remaining relative pairing
2: 
3: This working note isolates proved local maps from the still uncomputed
4: global relative Pontryagin pairing. It does not assign a return class
5: to the arbitrary fan. The two OS49 fans are already settled by P3K.17.
6: 
7: ## 1. The actual compact structure group and its loop
8: 
9: Use Cox coordinates \((a_0,a_1,c_0,c_1)\), auxiliary weights
10: \((1,1,-1,-1)\), and the moment coordinate
11: \(\mu=|a|^2-|c|^2\). The compact group preserving this data is
12: \[
13:  G=(U(2)_a\times U(2)_c)/S^1_{\rm aux}.
14: \]
15: The determinant degrees identify
16: \[
17:  \pi_1G=\mathbb Z^2/\langle(2,-2)\rangle.
18: \]
19: The actual bounded Cox parameter loop has weights
20: \((-A-B,0,A,B)\), for integral \(A,B\). Its determinant-degree
21: pair is \((-A-B,A+B)\), hence its class is in the order-two
22: subgroup and is nonzero there exactly when \(A+B\) is odd.
23: It is not legitimate to contract every such loop in \(G\).
24: 
25: ## 2. An exact twistor extension, including the odd loop
26: 
27: Conjugate the two auxiliary-negative coordinates. The inner boundary
28: of the real master space becomes ordinary \(\mathbb {CP}^3\),
29: and the parameter loop has projective weights
30: \((-A-B,0,-A,-B)\). Multiplication by the common scalar
31: \(\exp(\pi i(A+B)t)\) gives the determinant-one lift
32: \[
33:  \operatorname{diag}\bigl(
34:  e^{-\pi i(A+B)t},e^{\pi i(A+B)t},
35:  e^{\pi i(B-A)t},e^{\pi i(A-B)t}\bigr).                 \tag{FSpin.1}
36: \]
37: The two consecutive pairs are reciprocal. Thus (FSpin.1) lies in
38: \(Sp(1)\times Sp(1)\subset Sp(2)\) for the quaternionic
39: structure pairing the first two and last two coordinates. At
40: \(t=1\) it is the common central scalar
41: \((-1)^{A+B}\), so its projectivization is a genuine loop
42: in \(PSp(2)=SO(5)\). This is an equality of the actual
43: projective transformations, not only a homotopy of loops.
44: 
45: Let
46: \[
47:  Z=D(\Lambda^+TS^4),\qquad
48:  \partial Z=S(\Lambda^+TS^4)=\mathbb {CP}^3.             \tag{FSpin.2}
49: \]
50: The boundary equality is the twistor map sending a unit self-dual
51: two-form to its orthogonal complex structure on the oriented
52: tangent four-plane. Equivalently, the quaternionic Hopf projection
53: \(\mathbb {CP}^3\to\mathbb {HP}^1\) is the displayed sphere
54: bundle. The \(SO(5)\) action on \(S^4\) acts by its derivative
55: on \(\Lambda^+TS^4\), and (FSpin.1) is exactly its action on
56: the boundary. It therefore extends over the disk bundle at every
57: parameter value, with the same endpoints.
58: 
59: This action preserves an actual spin lift. At a point of the zero
60: section the isotropy representation is
61: \[
62:  SO(4)\longrightarrow SO(7),\qquad
63:  T Z|_{S^4}=\mathbb R^4\oplus\Lambda^+\mathbb R^4.
64: \]
65: Lift a generator of \(\pi_1SO(4)\) to a path in
66: \(Spin(4)=Sp(1)\times Sp(1)\). Its endpoint is
67: \((-1,-1)\). The standard four-plane contributes the central
68: minus one in \(Spin(4)\), while the self-dual three-plane,
69: through the first \(Sp(1)\), contributes the central minus
70: one in \(Spin(3)\). Their product in \(Spin(7)\) is plus
71: one. Hence the isotropy representation itself lifts to a
72: homomorphism \(SO(4)\to Spin(7)\). The homogeneous construction
73: \(SO(5)\times_{SO(4)}Spin(7)\), extended over the disk fibres
74: using an invariant connection, supplies the required
75: \(SO(5)\)-equivariant spin structure. In particular the odd
76: loop acts with a closed spin lift.
77: 
78: The orientation can be chosen so that the boundary is the standard
79: complex-oriented \(\mathbb {CP}^3\). With
80: \(u\in H^4(S^4;\mathbb Z)\), \(\int u=1\), one has
81: \[
82:  e(TS^4)=2u,\quad p_1(TS^4)=0,\quad
83:  p_1(\Lambda^+TS^4)=4u,\quad p_1(TZ)=4u.
84: \]
85: The sphere-bundle pullback is \(\pi^*u=h^2\), matching
86: \(p_1(T\mathbb {CP}^3)=4h^2\).
87: 
88: ## 3. The interface framing transgression is zero
89: 
90: The common truncated conifold interface is
91: \(K=(S^3\times S^3)/S^1_{\rm aux}\cong S^2\times S^3\).
92: Choose a stable framing \(\tau\) and include the invariant
93: radial, moment, and auxiliary directions in its stable tangent
94: comparison. For a based loop \(g_t\) in the actual compact
95: group define
96: \[
97:  F(x,t)=\tau(g_t x)^{-1}Dg_t\tau(x).
98: \]
99: At both endpoints this is the identity. The actual Cox loop has
100: a closed stable spin lift: before the auxiliary quotient its
101: four complex weights sum to zero, so its real spin lift is
102: closed; the three added invariant real directions do not change
103: that lift. Thus \(F\) is a based map
104: \(\Sigma K_+\to Spin\). Concatenation of loops concatenates
105: these maps, and a homotopy of loops gives a homotopy of the
106: derivative comparisons. Consequently this construction is a
107: homomorphism on the relevant fundamental-group classes.
108: 
109: The suspension splitting is
110: \[
111:  \Sigma(S^2\times S^3)_+
112:  \simeq S^1\vee S^3\vee S^4\vee S^6.
113: \]
114: Stable spin homotopy in those dimensions is respectively
115: \(0,\mathbb Z,0,0\). Hence the target group is torsion-free.
116: The actual Cox class has order at most two, so its derivative
117: comparison is nullhomotopic. This proves that the local
118: \(S^2\times S^1_{\rm parameter}\) stable-framing transgression
119: is zero even for the odd loop. The assertion includes the
120: specific spin lift and the specified boundary marking.
121: 
122: ## 4. The actual family bordism and the uncomputed global number
123: 
124: The real master space
125: \((\mathbb C^4\setminus0)/S^1_{\rm aux}\), truncated as in
126: P3K.17, has two small-resolution faces, their common outer face,
127: and the inner \(\mathbb {CP}^3\) face. Its stable tangent
128: bundle plus the auxiliary real direction is the associated sum
129: of the four complex weight lines. The sum of their weights is
130: zero, so it has a compatible spin structure. Glue (FSpin.2) to
131: the inner face using the exact twistor boundary map. Its unique
132: boundary spin structure agrees with that of the master space.
133: All maps carry the actual parameter loop by (FSpin.1), so this
134: constructs a spin bordism of the actual local families. Attach
135: the common exterior family times the bordism interval. This
136: gives a spin eight-dimensional bordism between the two actual
137: seven-dimensional mapping tori. Its projection to the parameter
138: circle has seven-dimensional cobordism fibres.
139: 
140: The signature is zero by the mapping-torus intersection
141: filtration: the classes containing the base-circle factor are
142: isotropic, and the complementary graded piece pairs with them.
143: The middle pairing is thus metabolic after quotient by its
144: radical. This statement retains boundary-relative cohomology;
145: the two boundary mapping tori have zero rational cohomology in
146: degrees three and four, so the absolute and relative middle
147: pairings identify in the required degrees.
148: 
149: The remaining calculation is the relative square of
150: \(p_1(TW)\). A double parameter cover contracts the local
151: \(G\)-loop, and Section 3 removes the local stable-framing
152: transgression. These facts do not by themselves identify the
153: whole exterior family with a product. Its fibre \(V\) is
154: homotopy equivalent to \(S^3\), but an exterior return fixed
155: on the boundary can still have a relative variation
156: \(H_3(V,\partial V)\to H_3(V)\). The additional boundary
157: group from \(S^2\times S^1\) must therefore be checked in
158: the relative gluing, rather than removed by the absolute
159: identity \(\pi_4SO=0\).
160: 
161: One concrete route is to use the actual torus-fibration tangent
162: transition matrices outside the critical cusp graph: their
163: graded factors are pulled back from the marked base and their
164: parameter dependence is off-diagonal. A common connection or
165: an explicit relative stable-framing extension through the
166: remaining toric critical curves would compute the missing
167: pairing. No vanishing of that global relative square is claimed
168: here until that extension is written.
